course: religion-ethics
lesson: 48
title: Lifeboat choices
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A dilemma is a hard choice where every option costs something: a lifeboat with room for only some people, one dose of medicine and two sick people, one place left on the school trip.
explain: Ethics asks not only "what should we choose?" but "how should we choose?": a fair way, such as drawing lots, a waiting list or helping the one in greatest need, can be better than picking favourites.
explain: Two kinds of thinking often meet here: rules thinking ("some things you must never do") and consequences thinking ("do what leads to the best results"), and they do not always agree.
explain: Christians see Jesus giving his life for others as the greatest example of self-sacrifice; it is admired, but nobody is required to be a hero.
explain: Sometimes there is no perfect answer; then a child today can still choose the least bad option, give honest reasons and treat everyone with respect.
fact: A dilemma is a choice in which every option has a cost.
fact: In the lifeboat dilemma there is room for only some people, and ethics asks which ways of choosing are fair.
fact: Fair procedures for sharing something scarce include drawing lots (picking names out of a hat), a waiting list and helping those in greatest need.
fact: Choosing only the "most useful" people is often criticised as unfair, because every person has equal worth.
fact: The trolley problem pictures a runaway tram (a trolley) heading for five people, and a lever that would switch it onto a track where it would hit one; it asks whether it is right to let one person be harmed to save five; people still disagree about it.
fact: Rules thinking and consequences thinking often give different answers to the trolley problem.
fact: Many people think there is a difference between doing harm and allowing harm, though this is disputed.
fact: "Women and children first" was an old rule for shipwrecks; people still discuss whether it is fair.
fact: Many people think the most vulnerable, such as small children, should be protected first.
fact: A group that must live together, as on a desert island, needs fair rules and leaders who serve everyone.
fact: Christians see Jesus' death for others as the greatest example of self-sacrifice.
fact: Self-sacrifice is admired, but it is usually seen as going beyond duty, not something everyone is required to do.
fact: Some dilemmas have no clearly right choice; then we look for the least bad option and give honest reasons.
exercise: What is a dilemma? | A choice in which every option has a cost | A question with one easy, obvious answer for everyone | A game where the winner gets a prize at the end | A rule that everyone always agrees with straight away
exercise: A lifeboat has room for only some people. Is drawing lots fairer than choosing the "most useful" people? | Many say yes, because everyone has an equal chance and equal worth | No, because only rich people should get a place | No, because the captain's friends should go first | Yes, because lots are always lucky for children
exercise: One place is left on the school trip and three children want it. What is the fairest way to give it out? | A fair method agreed in advance, like a draw or a waiting list | Give it to the child who shouts the loudest and longest about it | Give it to the teacher's favourite pupil | Give it to whoever pushes to the front first
exercise: There is one dose of medicine and two sick people. What should be considered? | Who needs it most, who it would help most, and a fair way to decide | Which of them is more famous on the television or has more followers online | Which of them has the nicer clothes | Which of them the doctor likes better
exercise: What does the trolley problem ask? | Whether it is right to let one person be harmed to save five | Whether trolleys should be allowed in supermarkets at busy times | Whether five people can fit in one trolley | Whether it is fair to push a trolley fast
exercise: How do rules thinking and consequences thinking differ? | Rules thinking says some acts are never allowed; consequences thinking looks at results | Rules thinking is for adults and consequences thinking is for children | They are exactly the same, with different names | Rules thinking ignores people; consequences thinking ignores numbers
exercise: Is there a difference between doing harm and allowing harm? Which answer shows good thinking? | Many say yes, as doing harm is worse, but some disagree, so it is disputed | No, and anyone who thinks so is just stupid | Yes, because allowing harm is never anybody's fault, whatever the situation | It doesn't matter, because harm always happens anyway
exercise: Why might the youngest be saved first in a shipwreck? | They are the most vulnerable and least able to save themselves | They are the most useful at rowing the boat | They are the lightest, so saving them makes the lifeboat go much faster | It is a rule nobody has ever questioned
exercise: The rule "women and children first" is still discussed today. Why? | Some ask whether it is fair to everyone, while others defend protecting the vulnerable | Because nobody knows what the rule says any more | Because it was a rule only for football matches | Because everyone agrees it is completely right
exercise: Your house is on fire. You can save your pet or a painting worth millions that could be given to charity. Which answer gives the best reasons? | Weigh your duty to the pet that trusts you against the good the money could do, and explain your choice | Save the painting, because money is the only thing that matters | Save the pet, because paintings are boring | Save neither, because thinking about it is too hard
exercise: Children are stranded on a desert island. Which first rule is most sensible? | Share food and water fairly so everyone survives | The strongest child on the island gets all the food | Nobody is allowed to talk to anyone else on the island | Whoever finds the island first owns everyone on it
exercise: What makes a good leader on the island? | One who serves the whole group and listens to everyone | One who gives orders all day and never explains any of them | One who takes the best food and the best shelter for themselves | One who was chosen just because they are the tallest of all
exercise: Is self-sacrifice a duty or something beyond duty? | It is usually seen as beyond duty: admired, but not demanded of everyone | It is a duty for every person every day | It is never good to give anything up for others | It is only for people who have a lot of money
exercise: What do Christians see as the greatest example of self-sacrifice? | Jesus giving his life for others | Noah building the ark in the desert | Jonah running away from God | King Solomon building the temple
exercise: How can you act when there is no perfect answer? | Choose the least bad option and give honest reasons | Refuse to choose at all, so that nobody can ever blame you | Choose at random and never explain to anyone why you chose it | Do whatever your best friend is doing, whatever it is
exercise: Is there always a right answer in ethics? | Not always; some dilemmas have no clearly right choice | Yes, and the right answer can always be found in a book | Yes, the answer is whatever most people want | No, so ethics is a waste of time
test: Your friend says, "In a lifeboat you should save the cleverest kids, cos they're worth more." What is the best reply? | Everyone has equal worth, so a fair method like drawing lots is better | Clever kids are boring, so save the sporty ones because they can swim better | You're right, cleverness is all that matters | Save whoever is your friend, that's fair
test: Brother: "Only one of us can go to the match, I'm older so it's mine, end of." Which response is fairest? | Agree a fair way, like taking turns or tossing a coin | He is older, so he must always get everything he wants | Hide his shoes and his coat so that he cannot go to the match | Shout and cry until Mum gives the ticket to you instead
test: "trolley problem's stupid, just pull the lever, 5 is more than 1 duh." What is weak about this? | It ignores the other side: some think doing harm is different from allowing it | It uses too much maths for an ethics question | It is too long and careful | Nothing; counting is always the full answer
test: A judge asks: "In the burning house, why save the pet?" Which answer is best-reasoned? | It trusts me and depends on me, though I know the money could help many people | Because I said so, and that is my answer | Because the other team's answer was silly | Because paintings can't feel and charity doesn't matter
test: A classmate of no religion says, "Self-sacrifice is just for Christians." What is a fair answer? | People of every faith and none admire giving things up for others | Only Christians are ever really allowed to be kind and give things up | Self-sacrifice is wrong for non-Christians | Only soldiers have ever made a sacrifice
test: Your cousin says, "Jesus died because he was caught by accident." How would a Christian put it right? | Christians believe Jesus gave his life willingly for others | Christians believe Jesus never died at all | Christians believe Jesus died of old age at home in his own bed | Christians believe Jesus died in a shipwreck
test: On a camping trip the older kids take all the marshmallows. "Tough, we're bigger." What would fair rules say? | Share them fairly, as being bigger gives no extra right | Bigger kids always get more, that's just nature and how it works | Nobody should have marshmallows at all on camping trips | The one who opened the bag owns all of them, every last one
test: "It's too hard, there's no right answer, so I'll just do nothing." What is wrong with this? | Even with no perfect answer we can choose the least bad one | Nothing; doing nothing is always the safest choice in the end | It is wrong only because the teacher will be cross | It is right, because hard questions don't count
test: Which statement about the trolley problem is true? | People still disagree about it, and it is a disputed question | Everyone agrees you must always pull the lever and save the five | Everyone agrees you must never pull the lever | It was solved long ago and nobody discusses it
test: "Last spot on the trip should go to Sam, he's my mate." What weak argument is this? | Favouritism: it ignores fairness to the others | A strong argument, as friends come first in fairness | An argument from need, which is always fair | A lottery, which is fair to everyone
test: Who should get the one medicine, a strong adult or a very sick child? Which answer reasons best? | Consider who needs it most and who it would help most, and choose fairly | The adult, because adults are always more important | The child, because the adult is rude | Whoever gets to the doctor's door first should have it, no questions asked
exam: Which of the following procedures is most generally regarded as fair for the allocation of a single remaining place among several equally entitled claimants? | The drawing of lots, every claimant thereby enjoying an equal chance | The preference of the claimant most favoured by the allocator | The award of the place to the claimant of greatest physical strength | The award of the place to whichever claimant first applies force
exam: Identify the statement that most accurately characterises the dilemma commonly termed the trolley problem. | It concerns whether harm to one person may be permitted in order to save five, and remains disputed | It concerns the conveyance of goods within a retail establishment | It concerns a question upon which all moral philosophers are agreed | It concerns the number of persons a vehicle may lawfully carry
exam: Pursuant to Christian teaching, select the response that most accurately states the place of self-sacrifice in the moral life. | It is admired as exceeding duty, the death of Jesus being its supreme example | It is a strict duty incumbent upon every person in every circumstance whatsoever | It is prohibited, being contrary to the care of oneself | It is held to be of no moral significance whatsoever
exam: Where a dilemma admits of no clearly correct resolution, determine the course which sound ethical reasoning recommends. | The least injurious option is to be chosen and the reasons honestly stated | All action is to be withheld so that no responsibility may attach to anyone | The choice is to be made at random without explanation | The choice is to be delegated to whichever person is most popular
